  "formal_question": "A rectangle is formed from 4 equally sized smaller rectangles. The shorter side is $10 \\mathrm{~cm}$ long. How long is the longer side?\nChoices:\nA:$40 \\mathrm{~cm}$\nB:$30 \\mathrm{~cm}$\nC:$20 \\mathrm{~cm}$\nD:$10 \\mathrm{~cm}$\nE:$5 \\mathrm{~cm}$",
  "material_difference": "Prior Plane283 was screened with zero saved designs/calls because whole contour emphasis added no meaningful correspondence. New three open existing edges explicitly bind the dimensioned full height to the two short end widths, leaving the actual middle10cm width and horizontal split visible. This is a long-versus-short unit transfer, not whole-outline salience or a color rename.",
  "primary_unique_math_proof": "Let small rectangle long side L and short side s. The two central horizontal rectangles are stacked across the whole height, so L=2s. The outer height is the long side L=10cm, hence s=5. Outer length is s+L+s=5+10+5=20cm, C uniquely. Falsely giving the two short red receivers the same numeric10 as the full-height red source makes10+10+10=30cm=B. Coloring carries no equality tick or condition and cannot override congruent rectangles and the explicit full-height dimension.",
  "human_semantics": "Numerical lengths of four congruent rectangles. Open edge color does not redefine the10cm dimension, which still spans the full outer height with unchanged dashed line and connectors. All four rectangle boundaries remain visible; no physical artwork transformation or new equality is requested.",
